 PUTCHAR:,NAME,
C===========================================================
C putchar(c) - write one character to *OUTPUT*.
C
C Calling convention for a one-argument ASSEMBLER routine:
C   ACC = c, the character
C   M13 = return link
C
C   void putchar(char) assembler;
C
C P/CW writes the character in ACC to the FILE addressed by
C M12 and returns through M13, so an ordinary character costs
C no more than the instruction that names *OUTPUT* and a tail
C call into it.
C A newline is not a character to put: PASEOLSY holds the one
C that ends a line, and a c equal to it goes to P/WOLN, which
C terminates the *OUTPUT* line the way puts does through P/WL.
C The character comes back by XORing a second time: the RMR the
C AEX left it in does not survive the branch, which writes the
C accumulator there itself.
C Both return through M13, so either way the tail call carries
C the caller's link.
C It is what a program uses where a whole printf, format and
C all, would cost more than the character it prints.
C===========================================================
 P/CW:,SUBP,                  . write one character, no width
 P/WOLN:,SUBP,                . terminate the line on *OUTPUT*
 *OUTPUT*:,LC,30              . 30-word FILE record of OUTPUT
 PASEOLSY:,LC,1               . end-of-line char (ASCII LF)
 ,UTC,PASEOLSY                . C := address of the LF character
 ,AEX,                        . zero exactly when c is the LF
 ,UZA,P/WOLN                  . it is: end the line, P/WOLN names *OUTPUT*
 ,UTC,PASEOLSY
 ,AEX,                        . the character back again
 12,VTM,*OUTPUT*
 ,UJ,P/CW
 ,END,
